    "recommendations": "Reducing the density of shrub cover and ground vegetation within the immediate structure perimeter would improve defensible space and reduce opportunities for fire to reach the structure. Where brush is growing in direct contact with or close proximity to the structure, creating separation between that vegetation and the building could meaningfully reduce structure exposure. Thinning or removing concentrated shrub growth near the structure to create horizontal separation between fuel masses may reduce fuel continuity across the property. Managing vegetation along the access route, including trimming encroaching shrubs and ground cover, would support firefighter access and improve conditions for suppression operations. Maintaining cleared areas around the structure and working progressively outward to reduce the density of mixed shrub and ground vegetation could improve overall defensible space over time. Continued vegetation management within the area surrounding the structure, particularly where dense brush connects directly to the building, would support long-term wildfire resilience."
